{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>An asham it is.</b> Rashi cites the teaching regarding an asham whose owners died, etc. — meaning the owners did not manage to offer it before they died, or the owners had already achieved atonement. In other words, this asham was lost while still alive, and its owner brought another [in its place], and afterward the first was found. Even though this [animal] stands to have its monetary value go up as an olah to the treasury of the altar — if he slaughtered it without specification, it is not valid as an olah until it was designated for grazing. The interpretation: they handed it over to a shepherd, and slaughtered it without specification — it is valid as an olah. And the term קיץ is like a person eating figs at the conclusion of a meal; thus Rashi explained in the chapter 'HaChalil.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:22.105513037Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}